<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>Garajul Meu</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	    Answers App Store Connect's export compliance question in the build, so
	    it is not asked again at every upload. False because the application
	    uses no encryption of its own: HTTPS through the system, the Keychain
	    through the secure-storage plugin, and push through APNs, all of which
	    Apple treats as exempt. The push token is encrypted at rest on the
	    server, not here. TRIGGER for revisiting: any cryptography added to the
	    application itself, such as encrypting a file on the device.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	    Two strings Capacitor does not add, and their absence is a crash rather
	    than a refusal. iOS terminates the process the instant an application
	    touches the camera without NSCameraUsageDescription, or the photo library
	    without NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription. Not a denied permission, not an
	    error a catch block sees - the app is gone. Three screens reach for both.

	    This is the iOS twin of POST_NOTIFICATIONS on Android, found the same way:
	    by reading what the plugin actually declares rather than what the
	    documentation says an application should call.

	    NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription is declared, and this application still
	    never writes to the library: nativeCamera.ts passes saveToGallery false.
	    It was deliberately absent until 2026-09-14. The gallery now goes through
	    the plugin's getPhoto, because chooseFromGallery hands over HEIC originals
	    the server refuses, and getPhoto rejects the call before showing anything
	    unless all three of these keys exist - read in CameraPlugin.swift,
	    checkUsageDescriptions. Declaring a purpose asks the user nothing; iOS
	    shows the string only if the application asks to add a photo, which it
	    does not. Remove it together with getPhoto, see the TRIGGER in
	    nativeCamera.ts.

	    WRITTEN IN ROMANIAN, and it is a known gap rather than a choice made
	    lightly. These are user-facing strings in a project whose rule is that no
	    such string is hardcoded in one language, and iOS reads them from the
	    bundle rather than from i18next - localising them needs InfoPlist.strings
	    and Xcode localisation, which cannot be verified from the machine this was
	    written on. Romanian because the application is Romanian-first and the
	    backend defaults new accounts to RO.
	    TRIGGER: the first English-speaking user, or the App Store listing being
	    prepared in a second language.
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Pentru a fotografia talonul mașinii și a completa datele automat, sau pentru a adăuga o poză vehiculului.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Pentru a alege o fotografie a talonului sau a mașinii din pozele tale.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Garajul Meu nu salvează nimic în pozele tale. Doar citește fotografia pe care o alegi tu.</string>

	<!--
	    Stops iOS from raising its own limited-access alert automatically, each
	    time the application reaches the library while access is limited to
	    chosen photos. On 2026-09-11 the alert sent the developer to Settings,
	    and changing a privacy setting terminates the application, which looked
	    exactly like a crash.

	    Still needed after the gallery moved to getPhoto on 2026-09-14. Until
	    then this comment said the older picker needs no permission at all, and
	    that was wrong: getPhoto asks for library access before it opens the system
	    picker, and looks the chosen asset up in the library afterwards for its
	    metadata. Both are the library access this alert fires on.
	-->
	<key>PHPhotoLibraryPreventAutomaticLimitedAccessAlert</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
    <false/>
    <key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
    <dict>
      <key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
      <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
            <string>Default Configuration</string>
            <key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
            <string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
            <key>UISceneStoryboardFile</key>
            <string>Main</string>
        </dict>
      </array>
    </dict>
	</dict>
	<!--
		Stops Firebase minting an FCM token at launch and uploading it before
		anybody has been asked anything. The token is a durable identifier for
		this handset, and the default is to create one the first time the app
		runs.

		This is not a restriction on the feature. FirebaseMessaging.getToken()
		sets isAutoInitEnabled back to true before it asks - read at
		ios/Plugin/FirebaseMessaging.swift line 57 on 2026-09-05 - so the effect
		is only to move the minting from launch to the moment somebody has said
		yes on screen 18. reportDevice() is written around exactly that.

		Its Android twin is the pair of meta-data elements in
		android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml, and the two must agree.
	-->
	<key>FirebaseMessagingAutoInitEnabled</key>
	<false/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>armv7</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
